raw_response:
The schema contains eleven distinct entity groupings: one conflict event, two actor-role projections, one fatality effect, one event location, one coordinate point, four administrative locations, and one ISO alternate identifier.

The event is identified by event_id_cnty and maps to bsm:Action. event_date denotes the event’s start, not an observation or upload time, so it maps to bsm:eventStartDateTime; timestamp is explicitly an API upload timestamp and remains unmapped. disorder_type, event_type, sub_event_type, and civilian_targeting classify the action and therefore share bsm:classificationCode. notes maps to the specifically qualified action-description property rather than the generic additional-information property. actor1 and actor2 connect through bsm:involvedActor because the schema only says they are involved; it does not establish the resource/target qualifiers required by bsm:resourceObject or bsm:targetsObject.

actor1 and actor2 each evidence bsm:Actor entities, identified by their names. Their inter1 and inter2 fields explicitly give actor types and map to bsm:typeName. The associated-actor fields cannot safely create actors because cells may contain several semicolon-separated actors and the permitted transformations cannot split them.

fatalities supports a separate bsm:ActionEffect connected by bsm:hasResult. It has the constant effect description “Fatalities” and the reported count as bsm:effectQuantityNumber. No bsm:effectOnObject relation is asserted because the schema does not identify whose fatalities were counted.

The event’s named place is a bsm:Location identified by latitude and longitude. It connects to a separate bsm:AbsolutePoint through bsm:hasGeometry; this respects the qualifier difference between bsm:hasGeographicLocation, which leads from the action to a Location wrapper, and bsm:hasGeometry, which leads from that wrapper to geometry. Administrative hierarchy is represented with bsm:partOf from the event location through admin2, admin1, country, and region. admin3 is omitted because it is sparse and no conditional template can safely choose it instead of admin2.

The country’s iso field is sufficiently specific to evidence bsm:AlternateIdentifier: both the field name and schema description identify ISO 3166 as the scheme. This is not inferred merely from a generic code field.

All IRI transformations were checked against their referenced subjects: actor_{actor1}, actor_{actor2}, effect_fatalities_{event_id_cnty}, location_{latitude}_{longitude}, point_{latitude}_{longitude}, admin2_{iso}_{admin1}_{admin2}, admin1_{iso}_{admin1}, country_{iso}, region_{region}, and country_iso_identifier_{iso} are character-for-character identical at each reference. No unnecessary transformation key is included, and every class and property used appears verbatim in the ontology.

